WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:53.180
[Music]

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.300
[Jack hums and sings]

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.880
[Steve:] Get a load of the crooner.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.520
[Mechanic:] I've been getting it all morning.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.320
I had to turn on the air compression to tune him off.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.380
[Jack:] You guys just don't appreciate good music.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.920
[Steve:] You feel pretty good today, don't you, Jack.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.000
[Jack:] On top of the world.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.760
[Steve:] Darned if I can figure why a guy should feel so happy getting his hands back in the grease.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.080
[Jack:] You might figure different if somebody told you you couldn't get your hands greasy, Steve.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.140
Maybe you'll think it's funny, but lots of times, when I was lying in bed looking at the ceiling,

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:31.080
I used to dream up repair jobs, and then go through them step-by-step.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:36.940
Now that I've got my hands on some real engines again, I'm not kicking.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.980
[Mechanic:] What was it like there, Jack, in the hospital?

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:41.340
[Jack:] It was no picnic.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.880
[Mechanic:] I bet, but I mean--what, what, what is it like to have TB?

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.100
What happens, and how do you know when you have it?

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.380
[Jack:] Trouble is, you generally don't know.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.760
I didn't.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.960
[Music]

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.000
I felt tired all the time.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.320
Played out.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.100
I didn't cough.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.480
You can have TB without coughing.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.540
And I was taking some pills I'd heard about.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:11.940
I figured I'd snap out of it.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.160
I always had before.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.660
I didn't think too much of it.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:28.260
[Music]

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.440
Then I would sweat at night. Used to wake up ringing wet.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.440
[Music]

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.720
Helen worried about it.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.600
You know how women are.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:41.930
She wanted me to go to the doctor.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.300
I told her you don't get me to any doctor unless I'm really sick.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:56.240
I guess I never would have found out in time, if it hadn't been for that community x-ray program.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.460
You know, when that trailer came around, and a lot of us had those chest pictures made.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.240
The next thing I knew, Doc Whipple called up.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.440
That's our family doctor.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.300
He said, Jack, can you stop by the office soon?

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.900
I want to talk to you.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.960
I said okay.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.160
I still didn't tumble.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.520
Thought maybe he wanted to talk to me about the kids or something.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.380
When I did go, he let me have it easy.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:32.470
It seems the x-ray fellows had sent him the report on me because I'd given his name as my doctor.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:37.520
He said he didn't like the looks of it, and he wanted a bigger picture made.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:44.760
So, I went and got a big picture, and Doc Whipple went over it with a specialist.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.220
[Music]

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:50.500
That did it.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:56.260
That and some other tests they made. I had TB, and it was well underway.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.600
He said that I'd have to go to a hospital to get well.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.720
I told him, Doc, I can't.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.360
I can't stop working.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.300
What will happen to Helen and the kids?

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.860
That's the first thing you worry about...the money.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.860
But the doctor had an answer: what will happen to them if you don't get well?

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:21.860
Of course, he was right, but it's hard to see your way clear at first.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.800
I hated to go home that day.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:33.600
We had a little put by, savings bonds, but not enough to last a year.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.040
The doctor thought it might take about that long.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:41.700
I figured that we'd lose the house that Helen had worked so hard over,

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.480
and I was worried for the youngsters.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.060
I'd always planned for them to get more schooling than I did.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.300
I didn't know what to do.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:53.760
But Helen did.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.020
She said I was going to the hospital right away.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.200
She's a great hand at budgeting, that girl.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.720
She showed me that with the savings bonds

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.900
and taking some loans on the insurance, we could manage somehow,

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:14.200
and she said she wasn't too proud to ask for help from the agencies Doc Whipple had mentioned.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:20.780
A few days later, I went to the hospital right over at Churchill.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.760
A lot of people still think you have to go the mountains or the desert.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.600
That's debunked.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.080
Climate isn't the important thing.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.740
The reason for going to a tuberculosis hospital instead of staying home,

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:39.020
is that the whole set up is geared for one thing--treating tuberculosis.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:43.120
They have all the equipment there, and they know what they're doing.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.860
[Music]

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:50.040
I won't pretend that I liked it there.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.600
Nobody likes being in the hospital, but I'm sure glad I went.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.920
For one thing, I wasn't spreading my own TB germs to my family or friends.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:04.960
There was the best of care and the right kind of food.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:09.200
[Music]

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.380
And rest.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.220
Rest, rest.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:16.700
That's the main thing in beating tuberculosis, rest.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:21.020
[Music]

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.940
The doctor told me why that is.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:26.680
You get to know a lot about TB when you're in the hospital.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:34.700
You see, almost everybody has healthy lungs to begin with.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:38.220
You have to get TB germs from somebody else.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.300
Maybe from them coughing or sneezing or spitting.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:48.360
When you breathe in those germs, they may get a foothold in the lungs, just like seeds taking root.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:54.560
But the body fights back by growing a kind of covering around them so they can't spread.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:01.220
They call this a tubercle, and it's like a scar  that holds the germs in so they can't do any harm.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.400
Usually, you don't know that this is happening.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.560
Lots of people have tubercles, and they get along all right for years,

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:15.540
but then maybe the tubercle breaks down, or more likely, you breathe in some more germs again.

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:20.360
They may spread faster than the body can stop them.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:23.640
Then you're in real trouble.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:29.320
The TB gets into the air pockets of the lung and destroys them, makes holes.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:33.080
In the long run, this can kill you.

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:39.720
The reason you need so much rest in bed is that the lung doesn't have to move much,

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.840
and that makes it easier for the body to fight off the germs.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.520
I asked the doctor about operations.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:51.420
They have different ones they can use for different cases.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:55.080
No two cases of TB are just alike.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.280
For me, he planned a pneumothorax.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:02.000
This is one way of giving an infected lung a rest.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:06.400
They put a hollow needle through the chest wall and let sterile air into it.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.940
This collapses the lung.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:13.460
Then it doesn't have to work to breathe, so the healing begins.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:16.380
The other lung does all the breathing you need.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:21.380
Of course, in time, the air put in around the lung is absorbed,

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:24.580
but they can easily keep it collapsed with more air.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:31.680
I had a refill every so often.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:38.280
What surprised me was that it didn't cause any pain, though at times it was uncomfortable.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.280
They have drugs now, too, that help a lot in some cases.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:49.720
I was lucky enough to respond well to one of the new drugs, and I got regular shots of it.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:56.270
[Music]

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:00.550
There's another angle to this business of licking TB.

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:02.260
Maybe the hardest one:

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.240
your state of mind.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.360
I used to get depressed sometimes.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.620
You wouldn't be human if you didn't.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:13.300
It's easy to say "rest and don't worry,"

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.540
but you do worry, about a lot of things.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:19.300
Mostly, I worried about my family.

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:24.020
Then I wondered how it would be when I came back.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:27.520
Would I be able to handle my job again?

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:30.940
Would fellows like you accept me as an equal?

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:36.260
It sounds silly, but it's real enough at the time, and it's bad for you.

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:40.860
Unless your mind's easy, your body can't fight.

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:47.490
[Music]

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:51.190
Of course, one thing that snapped me out of it was when Helen came to visit.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:55.240
That's one advantage of being in a hospital near home.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:58.560
We had plenty to talk about, the kids and all.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:03.520
Seeing her would chase away the glooms, make me know I had to get well.

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:09.560
And the people in the hospital keep an eye on your mental state, too.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:11.140
They know.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:15.680
They sent in a rehabilitation man to talk to me about my work.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:22.380
I told him that I just wanted to be a mechanic again, and after talking to the doctors, he agreed to that.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:27.570
But he figured I ought to have an extra string to my bowl, just in case.

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:33.390
He was smart: he sent me a course on part service, inventory control,

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:37.730
and it was practical because I knew all about parts from the other end,

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:42.450
and I did feel easier knowing that I had something to fall back on.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:46.740
They keep a constant check on you.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:49.080
X-rays and other tests.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:54.880
Well, it seemed a long time, and then I found that I was getting better.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:00.060
The doctor told me I was making some headway and could get up for a little exercise.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:04.920
[Music]

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:13.220
Just a few steps at first, but I tell you,I was as pleased and proud as a kid with his first Christmas tree.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:20.280
They let you exercise by slow degrees.

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:26.180
Just a little more each day, but finally, I was able to navigate just as well as ever.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:28.580
That builds up your confidence.

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:34.780
[Music]

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:37.580
At last, I was ready to come back.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:45.080
Like I said, I won't pretend I liked it there, but the people in that hospital were swell to me.

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:50.180
It's true you have to cure yourself, but you can't do it without their help.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:57.260
Coming back was wonderful, but it's not a clean break.

00:11:57.260 --> 00:12:02.120
You just don't go charging out into the world with a chip on your shoulder.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.700
We had a little celebration the first night at home.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.680
Just the family, but not late.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:14.380
You have to make adjustments in the way you live.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:18.680
That's one word you get to know when you've had TB--adjustments.

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:22.120
So I remembered to get to bed on time.

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:28.950
[Music]

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:33.750
Like when you go back to work, you can't always handle fulltime at first.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.210
The boss was swell about that.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:41.440
He had talked to my doctor and fixed it so I could work in the parts department just mornings.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:46.420
[Music]

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:49.080
That was three months ago, remember.

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.300
That part-time job didn't tire me.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:55.720
So I was anxious to get back to the old job.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.480
There's another thing you learn to get used to when you come back.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:02.440
They warned me about it at the hospital.

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:08.710
Some people are afraid of you...think they're gonna catch TB if you get near them.

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:12.200
Can't say I blame them, but they're all wrong.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:18.400
They don't discharge you from a hospital and send you home until it's safe all around.

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:23.900
Of course, I was going regular to Doc Whipple for checkups.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:27.320
Last week, he gave me the word I was waiting for.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:32.000
Said I was well enough now to get back to the old job full time.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:36.830
Believe me, fellows, it's a wonderful feeling to know that you've come all the way back.

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:39.230
That you're a fulltime man again.

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:41.910
[Steve:] It's great to have you back fulltime, Jack.

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:43.750
See that you stay that way.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:45.480
[Jack:] See that you stay that way, too.

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:46.250
[Steve:] Who, me?

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:48.270
[Jack:] Yes, you. Both of you.

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:53.140
The main reason I told you my story was to sell you one idea:

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:59.080
get a physical examination regularly and a chest x-ray.

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:03.220
If you haven't had one for a year, make a date soon.

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:06.280
It's the smartest thing you can do.

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:08.760
I know what I'm talking about.

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:19.980
[Music]

